# miForge configuration. Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) and fill in.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Foundation task 9.4. **Every variable `backend/src/core/config.py` reads is listed
# here**, and the values below are the ones that work against `docker-compose.yml`'s
# dev services while the backend runs from source. In Kubernetes these come from
# `gitops/pilot/config.yaml` and the `miforge-secrets` Secret, never from a tracked
# file (ADR-020).
#
# ⚠ Variable names are the UPPER_SNAKE_CASE of the settings field names, with no prefix
# (`core/config.py` sets no `env_prefix`). A misspelled name is a SILENT DEFAULT, not an
# error, so copy rather than retype.

# --- core services ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host ports are 55434 and 56381, not 5432 and 6379: miLLM, miStudio and miDataworks
# are checked out side by side on one workstation and already hold the defaults. These
# match `docker-compose.yml`; change both together.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://miforge:miforge-dev@127.0.0.1:55434/miforge
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:56381/0

# --- artifact roots (ADR-004) -------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ THE FILESYSTEM IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH, NOT THE DATABASE. `manifest.json` beside a
# checkpoint is authoritative; PostgreSQL is a rebuildable index. So these three paths
# hold the product, and the production defaults (/data/checkpoints, /data/trainer-state,
# /data/parts) are absolute paths a developer's machine will not have. Point them
# somewhere local and WRITABLE.
#
# `PARTS_ROOT` is the one that reads like a cache and is not: an imported dataset part's
# manifest is the authority too, so it is artifact storage on the same footing as a
# checkpoint.
CHECKPOINT_ROOT=./.local-data/checkpoints
TRAINER_STATE_ROOT=./.local-data/trainer-state
PARTS_ROOT=./.local-data/parts

# --- optional siblings --------------------------------------------------------------
# **Optional siblings: leave either unset and miForge runs standalone.** (miDataworks'
# own line, copied in spirit, because it is the clearest one-line statement of ADR-010
# anywhere in the suite.)
#
# ⚠ THESE ARE DELIBERATELY PRESENT AND EMPTY. Empty means unset means
# absent-and-normal: `core/config.py` has a validator that maps an empty string to
# `None` precisely so a blank line here does not configure a sibling as the empty URL.
# Do not give either a "convenient" default — a default base URL turns an absent
# sibling into a connection error at an arbitrary later moment instead of a named
# refusal at the boundary, and it would quietly take the standalone path (BRD
# acceptance criterion 20) off the path it exists to pin.
#
# What each one buys, and what is lost without it:
#   MILLM_BASE_URL        Serving a produced checkpoint through miLLM, and scoring the
#                         test and retention sets through its /v1 surface. Without it,
#                         miForge scores in-process or through any other
#                         OpenAI-compatible endpoint — miLLM is one row beside vLLM,
#                         Ollama and OpenAI, never a named dependency (ADR-009).
#   MIDATAWORKS_BASE_URL  Dataset parts, versioned selector and grader configurations,
#                         and the human-review queue. Without it, parts come from files
#                         with their own provenance and the human-review selector
#                         refuses with `midataworks_not_configured` while every other
#                         selector works.
MILLM_BASE_URL=
MIDATAWORKS_BASE_URL=

# How miForge identifies itself to miDataworks' REST surface. That surface has NO bearer
# gate — identity is this header alone. Must match `^agent:[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,47}$` or
# the process refuses at startup rather than on the first part resolution.
#
# ⚠ THREE SURFACES, THREE CREDENTIALS, NONE SHARED: this header, miDataworks' MCP bearer
# token (not used by miForge), and miLLM's `trainer` token below.
MIDATAWORKS_AGENT=agent:miforge

# --- Hugging Face, read by `huggingface_hub` ITSELF and not by miForge -----------------
# ⚠ DELIBERATELY NOT `Settings` FIELDS. The library reads these from the environment
# directly, so mirroring them into `core/config.py` would be a second source of truth for
# a value miForge never consults -- and a Settings field nothing reads is the unreachable
# capability this project keeps finding, in configuration form. They are documented here
# because they change behaviour (where the cache lands, whether a gated repo resolves) and
# an operator needs to know they exist.
#
# Both EMPTY by default, and that is the standalone path: with no token, a public repo
# still resolves and a gated one refuses by name. A LAN-only install may have no outbound
# network at all, which `services/parts/acquire.py` already states.
HF_HOME=
HF_TOKEN=

# --- the optional hot-update mode (BRD-02 §5.10) ONLY -------------------------------
# ⚠ NEEDED BY NOTHING ELSE. §5.10 is the only part of miForge that requires a sibling,
# and ADR-022 fences it: its code lives under `training/hotupdate/`, the standalone test
# runs with that module absent, and nothing in the core path may reference a miLLM
# endpoint. Leave both blank unless you are actually running that mode.
#
# MILLM_TRAINER_TOKEN is a bearer token from a deployment secret carrying the role
# `trainer`. On a 401 or 403 a run STOPS and reports the identity as the cause — it does
# not retry, because a rejected credential will not become valid by repetition.
#
# ⚠ A MISSING OR WRONG TOKEN FAILS AS THE WRONG DIAGNOSIS, one heartbeat interval late.
# The §5.10 heartbeat is gated on this token, and a heartbeat rejected for a bad
# credential is indistinguishable from a dead trainer: miLLM marks the attachment stale
# and the run dies looking like a crash rather than failing loudly at attach.
MILLM_TRAINER_TOKEN=
# ⚠ NOT DECORATION. The audit label is what makes a weights change attributable in
# miLLM's log, so a token without one is a change nobody can attribute (ADR-020).
# It travels INSIDE the token — BRD-01's wire contract names exactly two headers,
# `Authorization` and `X-miLLM-Attachment` — so this is miForge's declaration of which
# label its token carries, and what lets a half-configured identity be refused by name
# before `attach` rather than discovered as a rejected call.
MILLM_AUDIT_LABEL=

# R-02.2's verification tolerance: miForge runs one forward pass over the model it rebuilt
# from miLLM's handles and compares LOG-PROBABILITIES over an explicit token set with what
# miLLM answers for the same prompt. A mismatch aborts the run before its first step.
#
# ⚠ THE DEFAULT IS PROVISIONAL, NOT MEASURED. 1e-2 in log-probability space, as the MAXIMUM
# ABSOLUTE DIFFERENCE over the compared token set — a starting point for measurement, to be
# replaced before §5.10's acceptance by the observed spread across repeated identical calls
# on unchanged weights, times a margin. Every recorded result says which it was. Do not
# raise it to make a run start: a widened tolerance means a wrongly reconstructed graph
# trains silently and every test score is against a different model than the one served.
# (It was not compared with `logits`: `/v1` returns log-probabilities and never logits, so
# the original wording was unperformable and both repositories corrected it.)
HOTUPDATE_VERIFICATION_TOLERANCE=0.01

# The §5.10 heartbeat's interval. ⚠ LEAVE IT UNSET: the interval comes from the attach
# response's own hint (BRD-01 R-01.30 returns the staleness window and a suggested
# interval), because the timeout it must beat is miLLM's configuration and a local constant
# drifts from it in silence. An override exists for an operator who must force one.
HOTUPDATE_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=

# Per-op timeouts. ⚠ COMMIT-END'S IS DELIBERATELY THE LONGEST: a late commit-end is
# ACCEPTED by miLLM (R-01.23a) and returns the parameter set to `clean`, while abandoning
# the bracket leaves it `torn` and serving REFUSED. Giving up early is strictly worse.
HOTUPDATE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
HOTUPDATE_COMMIT_END_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# miLLM's own commit watchdog (its R-01.22), held here only so a commit duration can be
# judged against the limit it will be cut off at — a duration without its limit cannot be
# judged. It is THE OTHER REPOSITORY'S configuration: 200 ms since miLLM db44fea
# (2026-10-16), ten times the measured p99.9 of 10,000 full-parameter commits on the RTX
# 3090, rounded up (was a provisional 2,000). Mirror miLLM's WEIGHTS_COMMIT_WATCHDOG_MS;
# raising it here changes nothing there.
HOTUPDATE_COMMIT_WATCHDOG_MS=200
# Warn above this fraction of the watchdog. A run whose commits creep toward it is the early
# warning that the watchdog is set too tight — and a commit that reaches it is declared torn,
# which is a serving outage.
HOTUPDATE_COMMIT_CREEP_FRACTION=0.5
# R-02.2's reconstruction check, run once before step 1 of an attached run: the rebuilt
# model's top-N next tokens for this prompt, scored by miLLM (/v1/completions scoring mode)
# over exactly that set and compared within HOTUPDATE_VERIFICATION_TOLERANCE. The tokenizer
# is the SERVING model's: unset means the resident model name miLLM reports at attach.
HOTUPDATE_VERIFY_PROMPT=The capital of France is
HOTUPDATE_VERIFY_TOKEN_SET_SIZE=15
HOTUPDATE_VERIFY_TOKENIZER=
# Retries of a step refused BEFORE commit-begin (nothing written; BRD AC7). An interrupted
# commit is never retried (R-02.31) - this does not apply to it.
HOTUPDATE_PRECOMMIT_RETRIES=1

# --- jobs (ADR-007) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ A MINIMUM INTERVAL BETWEEN HEARTBEAT WRITES, NOT A MAXIMUM GAP. A generation-bound
# test pass or a 2.4 GB save legitimately exceeds it. Anything deriving a liveness
# threshold from this number is wrong — read the janitor's threshold instead. A screen
# that got this backwards would show every healthy run as stalled during every save.
HEARTBEAT_MIN_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
# The janitor's lapse threshold: how long without a heartbeat before a run is reaped.
# PROVISIONAL. Nothing in the product can yet produce a defensible value, because the
# measurement that would — the longest observed heartbeat gap per run — does not exist.
JANITOR_LAPSE_SECONDS=900
# One run per card, held by a lease WITH AN EXPIRY. A bare boolean with no holder and no
# expiry is the anti-pattern (ADR-008).
GPU_LEASE_SECONDS=3600

# --- misc ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
ENVIRONMENT=dev

# --- F3: the judge-model selector and selection health -------------------------------
#
# These are read by `services/selectors/settings.py`, a separate pydantic-settings class
# over this same file. NOT promoted into `core/config.py`, deliberately: `core.Settings`
# sets `extra="ignore"` so the two coexist, there is still exactly one reader per value,
# and centralising them would churn a module another feature imports for no behavioural
# gain. They are listed HERE because that is the gap that mattered — a variable absent
# from this file is a variable nobody knows exists, and foundation task 10.1 promises a
# clean clone starts with no manual step beyond `.env`.

# The judge provider. EMPTY means no judge is configured, which is normal: the judge-model
# selector then reports itself unavailable BY NAME (`judge_provider_not_configured`) and is
# still listed rather than hidden. It must NEVER fall back to the model being trained —
# R-02.11, because a model grading itself is not a measurement.
JUDGE_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=
JUDGE_PROVIDER_MODEL=
JUDGE_PROVIDER_API_KEY=

# The judge's prompt template. EMPTY and the judge refuses `judge_template_not_configured`
# rather than inventing one — a judge template is a verdict policy, and a hard-coded
# default would be the fabricated value ADR-018 forbids.
#
# ⚠ OPEN: `selector-config-v1.json`'s JudgeModelSelector has `{kind, name, config_sha256,
# provider, threshold}` and NO template field, while FR-003.5 says the template comes from
# the configuration. So today a verdict policy lives in an operator env var instead of in
# the versioned, hash-cited configuration. Either the schema gains `prompt_template` or
# FR-003.5 is amended; raised, not silently resolved.
JUDGE_PROMPT_TEMPLATE=

JUDGE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S=5.0
JUDGE_READ_TIMEOUT_S=30.0
JUDGE_CONCURRENCY=4

# Selection health. Below this sample size the degenerate-selection signals are not
# raised at all: "accepted 2 of 2" is not evidence that a selector accepts everything.
SELECTION_MIN_N_FOR_SIGNALS=5
GRADER_VARIANCE_EPSILON=1e-9

# Request and payload ceilings.
EVALUATE_MAX_COMPLETIONS=200
SELECTOR_MAX_TEXT_CHARS=65536
REVIEW_BATCH_MAX=500
RULE_MAX_PATTERN_CHARS=512

# --- F2: the four signal sources and the three objectives ----------------------------
#
# THREE OF THESE ARE PRESENT AND EMPTY ON PURPOSE, AND A RUN IS REFUSED WITHOUT THEM.
# Each decides something no requirement states and that no loss curve would reveal:
#
#   SIGNAL_SFT_MASKING_POLICY   — which tokens the cross-entropy is taken over.
#                                 `completion_only` ignores prompt tokens; `whole_sequence`
#                                 counts every one. R-02.4 says "next-token cross-entropy"
#                                 and nothing about masking, and whole-sequence training on
#                                 prompt/completion data teaches the model to generate
#                                 PROMPTS. A default here would change the objective.
#   SIGNAL_DPO_REFERENCE_SOURCE — `frozen_copy` (a frozen parameter set in host memory;
#                                 measure the transfer on THIS node, both PCIe links are x4)
#                                 or `precomputed` (reference log-probabilities cached per
#                                 batch before the run, invalid the moment the reference
#                                 changes). BRD-02 §9 leaves the choice open, which is not
#                                 the same as leaving it absent: without a reference a
#                                 direct preference objective is not one, and the loss still
#                                 falls.
#   SIGNAL_PENDING_SHORTFALL    — `refuse_batch` or `short_batch` when a batch cannot fill.
#                                 **Neither admits a pending completion**: that is fixed by
#                                 the three-valued verdict and is not configurable.
SIGNAL_SFT_MASKING_POLICY=
SIGNAL_DPO_REFERENCE_SOURCE=
SIGNAL_PENDING_SHORTFALL=

# Generation through the provider (ADR-009). The in-process provider is the default and
# needs no endpoint and no sibling. The timeout bounds a BATCH's generation, never a
# request - a provider owns its own connect and read timeouts.
SIGNAL_GENERATION_CONCURRENCY=4
SIGNAL_GENERATION_TIMEOUT_S=120.0

# Bounds the WAIT for a human-review decision, in seconds, and produces only a count.
# There is no path from this deadline to an admission.
SIGNAL_PENDING_DEADLINE_S=0

# The direct preference objective's strength. Recorded on every batch: a loss figure is
# comparable with another only when both say their beta.
SIGNAL_DPO_BETA=0.1

# Reward (R-02.7). A group of fewer than two has no relative advantage by construction and
# is refused. The epsilon is explicit because "zero spread" in floating point is a choice,
# and it matches F3's so the two cannot disagree about one group.
SIGNAL_REWARD_GROUP_SIZE=4
SIGNAL_REWARD_GROUPS_PER_BATCH=4
SIGNAL_REWARD_VARIANCE_EPSILON=1e-9

# Ceilings. Checked BEFORE a generation call and refused with the bound named rather than
# truncated: truncation changes the training example without changing any hash.
SIGNAL_MAX_PROMPT_CHARS=65536
SIGNAL_MAX_COMPLETION_CHARS=65536
SIGNAL_BATCH_PAGE_SIZE_MAX=200

# --- dev- and test-time overrides; leave unset in production -------------------------
#
# NOT application configuration. Each is read directly rather than through Settings
# because it exists to point a test at a different tree, and a production deployment
# should never set one.
#
# MIFORGE_SCHEMA_DIR          — the vendored-contract directory. A control perturbs a byte
#                               of a TEMP COPY through this, never the real file.
# MIFORGE_MILLM_ROOT          — a sibling checkout, for the byte-identity sync test. Unset
# MIFORGE_MIDATAWORKS_ROOT      means the test SKIPS naming the obligation, which is better
#                               than pointing it at a wrong path and getting a meaningless
#                               red.
# MIFORGE_TEST_DATABASE_URL   — a PostgreSQL URL for the suite's own database. Unset runs
#                               on SQLite. CI sets it for the serial PostgreSQL job and
#                               leaves it unset for the parallel SQLite one: both engines
#                               run the whole tree, because running only one is a known
#                               blind spot either way.

# --- NOT in this file, and deliberately ----------------------------------------------
#
# F10's `MIDATAWORKS_TIMEOUT_S`, `MIDATAWORKS_RETRY_MAX` and `REVIEW_POLL_INTERVAL_S` were
# reported as owed here. They are **not environment variables** — they are named constants
# and constructor arguments in `clients/midataworks.py`, with no `os.environ` read anywhere
# in that module. Listing them would advertise settings nothing reads, which is the mirror
# of the defect this section fixes: a documented knob that turns nothing is worse than an
# undocumented one, because someone will set it and believe it took effect.

# --- F4: inference providers (R-02.49, ADR-009) ---------------------------------------
#
# A ROW PER CONFIGURED ENDPOINT, discovered by prefix. miLLM, vLLM, Ollama and the OpenAI
# API are all configuration values here, never code paths — which is the whole point of
# R-02.49: "a test path wired to one sibling by name is what made the whole document
# depend on it". Unset means absent-and-normal: with nothing set, the in-process provider
# is the only row and acceptance criterion 20 holds.
#
# <NAME> is any [a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,47}; a selector configuration names a provider by it.
PROVIDER_<NAME>_BASE_URL=
PROVIDER_<NAME>_MODEL=                   # REQUIRED beside a base URL; the pair half-set REFUSES
PROVIDER_<NAME>_API_KEY=                 # optional — a locally served model needs none
PROVIDER_<NAME>_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S=       # default 5.0; never None — a missing timeout is a hang
PROVIDER_<NAME>_READ_TIMEOUT_S=          # default 60.0
PROVIDER_<NAME>_CONCURRENCY=             # default 4
PROVIDER_IN_PROCESS_KV_BYTES_PER_TOKEN=  # default 131072; the KV-cache budget coefficient
PROVIDER_IN_PROCESS_BATCH_BOUND=         # default 8

# --- F4: test and retention scoring (R-02.16 – R-02.18) -------------------------------
SCORING_PROVIDER=                        # default in_process — the default that makes AC20 hold
SCORING_DECODE_TEMPERATURE=              # default 0.0 — GREEDY, the only case criterion 2 is exact for
SCORING_DECODE_TOP_P=                    # default 1.0
SCORING_DECODE_MAX_TOKENS=               # default 256
SCORING_DECODE_STOP=                     # comma-separated; empty means none
TEST_EVERY=                              # default 1
# Default 1 WHEN A FLOOR IS SET, and a coarser value reports its exposure window: with N,
# up to N-1 steps of training run on a model already below its floor. The operator's number
# is not chosen here — the window is stated so the choice is informed.
RETENTION_EVERY=
SCORING_BATCH_BOUND=                     # default 8
# Default 2000, but THE EFFECTIVE CEILING IS THE LOWER OF THIS AND F3's
# EVALUATE_MAX_COMPLETIONS (200). Two features held two numbers for one bound: a
# 201-prompt set resolved, GENERATED 201 completions, and only then failed at F3 —
# paying for generation before refusing, against R-02.48. The refusal now names which
# bound bit.
SCORING_MAX_PROMPTS=
# --- F4: the optional activation source (R-02.19, R-02.50, ADR-010) -------------------
#
# UNSET MEANS ABSENT-AND-NORMAL: with no base URL nothing on the scoring path reaches for
# it, and a test or retention pass completes with the capability absent and every score
# recorded. A BLANK VALUE IS ALSO UNSET — `ScoringSettings` coerces `""` to None, because
# the deliberately-blank line below would otherwise configure the source as the empty URL
# and the standalone path would never be taken on a developer's machine. Credentials in
# the URL are REFUSED, not masked: a base URL is a location, identity travels separately.
#
# *This row's comment read "NOT IMPLEMENTED: ... A test goes red when the module lands"
# until 2026-10-16. The module landed — `clients/activations.py`, one outbound call site —
# so the comment was telling an operator that a variable the code reads does nothing.*
ACTIVATION_SOURCE_BASE_URL=
# The probe ROUTE is deliberately NOT a variable here: `Probe.statistics` takes it from its
# caller and refuses `activation_source_route_unspecified` on an empty one, because no
# requirement in BRD-02 names a route for a sibling's probe endpoints and a default here
# would be an invented contract asserted as someone else's. Its timeout is likewise a
# constructor default (10.0 s) and not read from the environment — same reason the
# MIDATAWORKS_* note above gives for leaving those out: a documented knob that turns
# nothing is worse than an undocumented one, because someone will set it and believe it.
